---
import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';

const bodyContent = `<p>This tutorial takes you from zero to a working multi-agent network. Two agents, on different machines (or the same machine), communicating over encrypted tunnels with a mutual trust relationship. The whole thing takes about 5 minutes.</p>

  <p>By the end, you will have two agents that can send messages, transfer files, ping each other, and publish/subscribe to event streams -- all over Pilot Protocol's encrypted UDP overlay.</p>

  <h2 id="prerequisites">Prerequisites</h2>

  <p>The one-line installer ships prebuilt binaries — <strong>no Go toolchain required</strong>. (Building from source needs Go 1.25+.) The install drops a small set of binaries: <code>pilotctl</code>, <code>pilot-daemon</code>, and the auto-updater.</p>

  <p>You don't need to set up any servers. By default the daemon registers with the public registry and beacon that Vulture Labs runs at <code>34.71.57.205</code>, free for anyone.</p>

  <h2 id="install">Step 1: Install pilotctl</h2>

  <p>Install the CLI tool with a single command:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="cmd">curl -fsSL https://pilotprotocol.network/install.sh | sh</span></code></pre>

  <p>This installs the binaries to <code>~/.pilot/bin</code> and adds that directory to your <code>$PATH</code>.</p>

  <p>Verify the installation:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="cmd">$ pilotctl version</span>
1.18.0</code></pre>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Alternative:</strong> You can also clone the repository and build from source with <code>make build</code>, which builds both <code>pilotctl</code> and the daemon. See the <a href="/docs/getting-started">Getting Started guide</a> for details.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="start">Step 2: Start Two Agents</h2>

  <p>Each agent needs its own identity. The daemon generates a cryptographic identity (Ed25519 key pair) the first time it starts and stores it locally.</p>

  <h3>Agent Alice (Terminal 1)</h3>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># Start the daemon and register as "alice"</span>
<span class="comment"># (first start creates the identity in ~/.pilot/identity.json)</span>
<span class="cmd">$ pilotctl daemon start --hostname alice --email alice@example.com --public</span>
Daemon running (pid 48213)
  Address:  0:0000.0000.0003
  Hostname: alice
  Transport: udp
  Socket:   /tmp/pilot.sock
  Logs:     /home/alice/.pilot/pilot-48213.log</code></pre>

  <h3>Agent Bob (Terminal 2)</h3>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># On a different machine, start the daemon and register as "bob"</span>
<span class="cmd">$ pilotctl daemon start --hostname bob --email bob@example.com --public</span>
Daemon running (pid 51007)
  Address:  0:0000.0000.0004
  Hostname: bob
  Transport: udp
  Socket:   /tmp/pilot.sock
  Logs:     /home/bob/.pilot/pilot-51007.log</code></pre>

  <p>Both agents are now running. The <code>--public</code> flag makes them resolvable by hostname for anyone. Without it, agents are private and resolve only for trusted peers. (Your addresses will differ; the registry assigns them in order.)</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Same machine?</strong> If you are running both agents on one machine for testing, give the second one its own state directory and socket: run Bob's commands as <code>PILOT_HOME=~/bob PILOT_SOCKET=/tmp/pilot-bob.sock pilotctl ...</code> and start his daemon with <code>--socket /tmp/pilot-bob.sock</code>. The tunnel listen port is picked at random by default, so the two daemons don't collide.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="discover">Step 3: Discover and Establish Trust</h2>

  <p>Alice can find Bob by hostname because he registered as public:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># From Alice's terminal</span>
<span class="cmd">$ pilotctl find bob</span>
Hostname:  bob
Node ID:   4
Address:   0:0000.0000.0004
Visible:   public</code></pre>

  <p>Now Alice initiates a trust handshake. The justification message tells Bob why Alice wants to connect:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># Alice requests trust</span>
<span class="cmd">$ pilotctl handshake bob "Collaborating on the Q1 data analysis project"</span>
handshake request sent to node 4
  they must approve on their side · check status: pilotctl trust · propagation can take ~60s</code></pre>

  <p>On Bob's side, the handshake request appears. Bob reviews it and approves:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># Bob sees the pending request</span>
<span class="cmd">$ pilotctl pending</span>
Pending handshakes — 1

  node 3 · 12s ago (2026-02-28 10:14)
  Collaborating on the Q1 data analysis project
  accept: pilotctl approve 3 · decline: pilotctl reject 3 "reason"

<span class="comment"># Bob approves</span>
<span class="cmd">$ pilotctl approve 3</span>
trust established with node 3
  trust is now mutual — message them: pilotctl send-message 3 --data "hi"</code></pre>

  <p>Done. Alice and Bob now have a <strong>mutual trust relationship</strong>, signed with Ed25519 keys. An encrypted tunnel is established automatically.</p>

  <h2 id="communicate">Step 4: Communicate</h2>

  <p>With trust established, Alice and Bob can use the full set of communication primitives.</p>

  <h3>Ping</h3>

  <p>Test connectivity and measure latency:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="cmd">$ pilotctl ping bob</span>
PING 0:0000.0000.0004
<span class="comment"># then one line per reply (4 by default) with its round-trip time</span></code></pre>

  <h3>Send a Message</h3>

  <p>Send a message through the Data Exchange service on port 1001. Bob's daemon acknowledges it and saves it to his inbox:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># Alice sends a message to Bob</span>
<span class="cmd">$ pilotctl send-message bob --data "Analysis complete. 47 anomalies detected in dataset-7."</span>
{
  "ack": "ACK TEXT 54 bytes",
  "bytes": 54,
  "reused": false,
  "seq": 0,
  "target": "0:0000.0000.0004",
  "to": "0:0000.0000.0004",
  "type": "text"
}

<span class="comment"># Bob reads his inbox</span>
<span class="cmd">$ pilotctl inbox</span>
Inbox — 1 message(s) · showing 1 newest

  TEXT-20260228-101502.481-000001
  0:0000.0000.0003 · TEXT · 9s ago · 54 B
  Analysis complete. 47 anomalies detected in dataset-7.</code></pre>

  <h3>Transfer a File</h3>

  <p>Send files through the encrypted agent tunnel -- no S3 bucket or workflow-specific file broker:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># Alice sends a file to Bob</span>
<span class="cmd">$ pilotctl send-file bob ./report.pdf</span>
<span class="comment"># prints a JSON summary: filename, bytes, elapsed_ms, throughput_mbps, ack</span>

<span class="comment"># Bob receives files into ~/.pilot/received/</span>
<span class="cmd">$ pilotctl received</span></code></pre>

  <h3>Publish/Subscribe Events</h3>

  <p>Every daemon runs a pub/sub broker on port 1002. You subscribe to a topic on a peer's broker, and anything published to that topic on that broker reaches every subscriber. A daemon can't dial itself, so both commands target a peer's broker, here Alice's:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># Bob subscribes to topic "status" on Alice's broker</span>
<span class="cmd">$ pilotctl subscribe alice status</span>
subscribed to "status" on 0:0000.0000.0003 — waiting for events...

<span class="comment"># From a second terminal on Bob's machine (or any other trusted peer),</span>
<span class="comment"># publish to the same topic on Alice's broker</span>
<span class="cmd">$ pilotctl publish alice status --data "processing batch 1/10"</span>
<span class="cmd">$ pilotctl publish alice status --data "processing batch 2/10"</span>

<span class="comment"># The subscriber sees them in real time</span>
[status] processing batch 1/10
[status] processing batch 2/10</code></pre>

  <h2 id="explained">What Just Happened</h2>

  <p>Behind those simple CLI commands, a surprising amount happened:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identity generation</strong> -- the daemon generated an <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8032.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ed25519</a> key pair on first start and stored it in <code>~/.pilot/identity.json</code>. This key pair is the agent's permanent cryptographic identity.</li>
    <li><strong>STUN discovery</strong> -- when the daemon started, it sent a UDP packet to the beacon server and learned its own public IP:port mapping. This is how other agents can reach it through NAT.</li>
    <li><strong>Registration</strong> -- the daemon connected to the registry and registered its hostname, public key, and STUN-discovered endpoint; the registry assigned its address.</li>
    <li><strong>Handshake</strong> -- Alice's handshake request was signed with her Ed25519 private key and relayed through the network to Bob. Bob verified the signature, approved, and signed his response. Both sides now store the peer's public key.</li>
    <li><strong>Tunnel establishment</strong> -- with trust established, Alice's daemon resolved Bob's endpoint from the registry, attempted direct UDP connection (using hole-punching if needed), and performed X25519 key exchange to set up AES-256-GCM encryption.</li>
    <li><strong>Communication</strong> -- every message, file, and ping traveled over the encrypted tunnel as Pilot Protocol packets with the 34-byte header, CRC32 checksums, sequence numbers, and acknowledgments.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>All of this happened without configuring firewalls, opening ports, setting up TLS certificates, or deploying your own servers. The agents found each other through the public registry, established trust, traversed NAT, and communicated. If you want to take this further, you can <a href="/blog/build-agent-swarm-self-organizes">build a self-organizing agent swarm</a>, <a href="/blog/replace-message-broker-twelve-lines-go">use the built-in pub/sub in place of a message broker</a>, or <a href="/blog/http-services-over-encrypted-overlay">run standard HTTP services over the encrypted overlay</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="next">Next Steps</h2>

  <p>Now that you have a working network, explore further:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><a href="how-pilot-protocol-works"><strong>How Pilot Protocol Works</strong></a> -- understand the full architecture: packet format, NAT traversal tiers, encryption, and trust model</li>
    <li><a href="trust-model-agents-invisible-by-default"><strong>The Trust Model</strong></a> -- learn how private endpoint resolution, peer handshakes, and network membership work</li>
    <li><a href="why-ai-agents-need-network-stack"><strong>Why Agents Need a Network Stack</strong></a> -- the motivation behind Pilot Protocol and why HTTP is not enough for agent communication</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/cli-reference"><strong>CLI Reference</strong></a> -- complete documentation for every <code>pilotctl</code> command</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/services"><strong>Built-in Services</strong></a> -- port-based services including Echo (7), Stdio (1000), Data Exchange (1001), and Event Stream (1002)</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/integration"><strong>Integration Guide</strong></a> -- embed Pilot Protocol in your Go applications</li>
    <li><a href="/blog/build-agent-swarm-self-organizes"><strong>Build a Self-Organizing Agent Swarm</strong></a> -- take multi-agent networking further with a group of agents that find each other, establish trust, and share work</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="cta">
    <h3>Explore the Full Documentation</h3>
    <p>CLI reference, Go API, configuration, deployment guides, and more.</p>
    <a href="/docs/">Read the Docs</a>
  </div>`;
---
<BlogLayout
  title="Build a Multi-Agent Network in 5 Minutes"
  description="Step-by-step tutorial: install Pilot Protocol, register agents, establish trust, and send your first encrypted message."
  date="February 28, 2026"
  tags={["tutorial", "quickstart", "getting-started"]}
  canonicalPath="/blog/build-multi-agent-network-five-minutes"
  bannerImage="/blog/banners/build-multi-agent-network-five-minutes.webp"
>
  <Fragment set:html={bodyContent} />
</BlogLayout>
